sis of Valid Moves:**\n\n1.  **Move (H, 1)**:\n    *   **Immediate Effect**: Places @ at H1.\n    *   **Captures**:\n        *   Vertically: G1 (*) is sandwiched by F1 (@). Flips G1 to @.\n        *   Diagonally: G2 (*) is sandwiched by F3 (@). Flips G2 to @.\n    *   **Strategic Value**: This move is crucial because it converts G1 and G2 to your color. Currently, the G row (G1-G6) consists of opponent pieces. By securing G2, you establish an anchor on the left side of this row.\n    *   **Future Consequence**: The opponent will be forced to play in the G7/G8 area.\n        *   If Opponent plays G7: You play G8. With G2 as an anchor, placing a piece at G8 will capture the entire sequence G3, G4, G5, G6, G7.\n        *   If Opponent plays G8: You play G7. Similarly, with G2 as an anchor, playing G7 captures G3, G4, G5, G6. Additionally, G7 will likely capture pieces in column 7 and the diagonal F6-E5.\n    *   **Result**: This sequence leads to flipping almost the entire G row and securing the bottom right corner area, leading to a maximal score.\n\n2.  **Move (G, 7)**:\n    *   **Immediate Effect**: Places @ at G7.\n    *   **Captures**: Diagonally captures F6 and E5.\n    *   **Strategic Value**: While it captures two pieces, it fails to utilize the G-row weakness.\n    *   **Future Consequence**: The opponent can reply with G8. This would capture your new piece at G7 (sandwiched by G6). You lose the position in the G row, and the opponent gains a foothold in the corner/edge. You miss the opportunity to flip the sequence G3-G6.\n\n**Conclusion**:\nPlaying (H, 1) is the superior move. It is a quiet setup move that prepares a devastating follow-up on the G-row. By securing G2 first, you ensure that no matter where the opponent plays next, you can sweep the G-row on your subsequent turn, guaranteeing a win by a large margin.\n\nEvaluation: A winning setup move that flips G1 and G2, creating an anchor to capture the entire G-row (G3-G6) on the next turn.\nReason: Playi